1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,920
Imagine a stock that has climbed for months,

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:05,430
from thirty two dollars all the way to sixty.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:08,630
It dips, then pushes to a new high of seventy.

4
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:11,549
But the next push only reaches sixty one.

5
00:00:11,549 --> 00:00:13,530
It could not even make a new high.

6
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:17,110
Then it falls through a level it had bounced off twice before.

7
00:00:17,910 --> 00:00:19,934
Traders have a name for this shape.

8
00:00:19,934 --> 00:00:23,080
It looks like a person: two shoulders and a head.

9
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,060
The head and shoulders pattern.

10
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:27,628
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

11
00:00:27,628 --> 00:00:29,870
Where math is something you can see.

12
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:33,530
Today you will learn how to spot the three peaks.

13
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:36,660
You will see why the neckline is the line that matters.

14
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:40,120
And you will measure a price target with simple subtraction.

15
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,890
Let's slow down and read the chart one peak at a time.

16
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:47,723
The first peak, at sixty, is the left shoulder.

17
00:00:47,723 --> 00:00:49,740
Buyers are still in control.

18
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:53,128
The second peak, at seventy, is the head.

19
00:00:53,128 --> 00:00:55,510
It is the highest point of the whole move.

20
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:59,282
The third peak, at sixty one, is the right shoulder.

21
00:00:59,282 --> 00:01:00,990
It is lower than the head.

22
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:03,970
That lower high is the first warning.

23
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:06,810
Buyers tried again, and they ran out of strength.

24
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:09,804
The shoulders do not need to match exactly.

25
00:01:09,804 --> 00:01:11,460
Real charts are never that neat.

26
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:14,054
Now look at the two dips between the peaks.

27
00:01:14,054 --> 00:01:15,690
Both stopped at fifty two.

28
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:18,640
Join them with a line, and you have the neckline.

29
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:22,975
Here is the key idea. Until price closes below the neckline,

30
00:01:22,975 --> 00:01:24,430
there is no pattern yet.

31
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,840
It is only a shape that might become one.

32
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:30,740
When the price closes below fifty two, the pattern is confirmed.

33
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,538
Volume often tells the same story.

34
00:01:33,538 --> 00:01:35,733
Trading is lighter on the right shoulder,

35
00:01:35,733 --> 00:01:36,940
then jumps on the break.

36
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:40,336
And sometimes the price comes back up to touch the neckline from

37
00:01:40,336 --> 00:01:42,210
below, before it falls away.

38
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:44,341
So how far might it fall?

39
00:01:44,341 --> 00:01:46,090
There is a simple rule of thumb.

40
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,850
Measure the height of the head above the neckline.

41
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,620
Seventy minus fifty two is eighteen dollars.

42
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:55,480
Now copy that same eighteen down from the neckline.

43
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:58,341
Fifty two minus eighteen is thirty four.

44
00:01:58,341 --> 00:01:59,810
That is the price target.

45
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:03,411
Traders also decide where they would be wrong.

46
00:02:03,411 --> 00:02:06,228
A common choice is just above the right shoulder,

47
00:02:06,228 --> 00:02:07,530
at sixty one.

48
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:12,037
Selling at the break, at fifty two, risks nine dollars to aim

49
00:02:12,037 --> 00:02:13,000
for eighteen.

50
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:17,090
That is two dollars of possible reward for every dollar of risk.

51
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:19,650
Three mistakes trip up beginners.

52
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,473
Mistake one: acting before the neckline breaks.

53
00:02:23,473 --> 00:02:26,220
Many right shoulders simply turn back up.

54
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:29,003
Mistake two: finding the shape anywhere.

55
00:02:29,003 --> 00:02:32,811
It is a reversal pattern, so it needs an uptrend before it to

56
00:02:32,811 --> 00:02:33,430
reverse.

57
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:36,998
Mistake three: treating the target as a promise.

58
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:39,754
It is a rule of thumb. Price can stop short,

59
00:02:39,754 --> 00:02:40,900
or overshoot.

60
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:45,621
And a bonus: flip the chart upside down, and you get the inverse

61
00:02:45,621 --> 00:02:48,890
head and shoulders, a possible bottom after a fall.

62
00:02:49,690 --> 00:02:50,890
Here is the whole recipe.

63
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:53,250
One. Start with an uptrend.

64
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:57,230
Two. Find three peaks, with the head the highest.

65
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:00,640
Three. Draw the neckline through the two dips.

66
00:03:00,990 --> 00:03:03,390
Four. Wait for a close below it.

67
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:08,197
Five. Measure the head, copy it down, and set where you would be

68
00:03:08,197 --> 00:03:08,670
wrong.

69
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:11,326
A pattern is not a prediction.

70
00:03:11,326 --> 00:03:13,000
It is a way to plan your risk.

71
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:17,743
Look around your world, and notice how often a rally runs out of

72
00:03:17,743 --> 00:03:18,940
steam before it turns.

73
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:21,556
If this lesson made things clear for you,

74
00:03:21,556 --> 00:03:24,371
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

75
00:03:24,371 --> 00:03:25,190
together.

76
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:27,480
But here's a puzzle for next time.

77
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:31,379
What if the price makes only two peaks, at the same height?

78
00:03:31,379 --> 00:03:32,710
That is the double top.

79
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:34,790
I will see you in the next video.
